---
import Piece from '../../../layouts/Piece.astro';
---

<Piece
  project="dyslexia-across-languages"
  title="feel the switch"
  headline="Your keyboard switches languages in one tap. Dyslexia does not."
  lede="Three short trials. Each one puts you inside a moment that bilingual people with dyslexia hit every day, in Farsi, Mandarin and English. About two minutes."
  description="Three two-minute trials: read Farsi without vowels, pick between look-alike Chinese characters, and type a mixed-language text while autocorrect fights you."
  next={{ href: '/work/dyslexia-across-languages/friction-map/', title: 'friction map' }}
>
  <section class="section trial" aria-labelledby="t1-h">
    <p class="trial-step mono">trial 1 of 3, farsi</p>
    <h2 id="t1-h" class="hl">Read this sentence out loud.</h2>
    <p>
      Everyday written Farsi leaves out most vowel marks. Readers fill them in from memory. Here is
      what that feels like in English.
    </p>
    <p class="task task-big">Ths s wht wrttn Frs fls lk. Th vwls r gn, nd y hv t gss thm.</p>
    <div class="trial-row">
      <button type="button" class="piece-btn" id="t1-btn">Show the vowels</button>
      <span class="trial-note" id="t1-time" aria-live="polite"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="reveal" id="t1-rev" hidden>
      <p>
        <strong>This is what written Farsi feels like. The vowels are gone, and you have to guess them.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>The real thing, first as people usually write it, then with vowel marks added:</p>
      <p class="task task-big fa" lang="fa">سلام، حالت چطوره؟ فردا میبینمت.</p>
      <p class="task task-big fa" lang="fa">سَلام، حالَت چِطورِه؟ فَردا میبینَمَت.</p>
      <p class="trial-note">Meaning: “Hi, how are you? See you tomorrow.”</p>
      <div class="finding">
        <b>What research says</b>
        Vowel-free Persian behaves like an opaque script: readers decode letter by letter, which is
        exactly the process dyslexia disrupts (Pouretemad et al., 2011). No keyboard or reading mode
        offers an optional vowel-mark view.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section trial" aria-labelledby="t2-h">
    <p class="trial-step mono">trial 2 of 3, mandarin</p>
    <h2 id="t2-h" class="hl">Pick the right character.</h2>
    <p>
      Typing Chinese on a phone means typing the sound in letters (pinyin), then choosing the
      character from a pop-up list. Every option below has the same sound.
    </p>
    <p class="task">You typed <strong>yǐ</strong>. You mean <strong>“already”</strong>. Which one is it?</p>
    <div class="choices" id="t2-a" role="group" aria-label="Characters for yǐ">
      <button type="button" class="choice zh" lang="zh" data-v="0">己</button>
      <button type="button" class="choice zh" lang="zh" data-v="1">已</button>
      <button type="button" class="choice zh" lang="zh" data-v="0">巳</button>
    </div>
    <p class="trial-msg" id="t2-m1" aria-live="polite"></p>
    <div class="reveal" id="t2-rev" hidden>
      <p>One more. You typed <strong>wèi</strong>. You mean <strong>“not yet”</strong>.</p>
      <div class="choices" id="t2-b" role="group" aria-label="Characters for wèi">
        <button type="button" class="choice zh" lang="zh" data-v="0">末</button>
        <button type="button" class="choice zh" lang="zh" data-v="1">未</button>
        <button type="button" class="choice zh" lang="zh" data-v="0">味</button>
      </div>
      <p class="trial-msg" id="t2-m2" aria-live="polite"></p>
      <div class="reveal" id="t2-rev2" hidden>
        <p>
          The difference between <span class="zh" lang="zh">己 已 巳</span> is a single stroke length.
          Between <span class="zh" lang="zh">末</span> and <span class="zh" lang="zh">未</span>, which of
          two lines is longer.
        </p>
        <div class="finding">
          <b>What research says</b>
          Dyslexia in Chinese involves the visual processing of character shape, and uses different
          brain regions than dyslexia in alphabetic scripts (Siok, Perfetti, Jin &amp; Tan,
          <em>Nature</em>, 2004). Pinyin input moves the whole difficulty into this one picker step,
          and heavy pinyin use is linked to weaker character reading (PNAS). The picker shows shapes
          only. It could show meanings.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section trial" aria-labelledby="t3-h">
    <p class="trial-step mono">trial 3 of 3, the switch</p>
    <h2 id="t3-h" class="hl">Text a Farsi-speaking friend, in English letters.</h2>
    <p>
      Many people write Farsi this way. Type the message below one word at a time, pressing space
      after each word. Autocorrect is set to English. Watch what it does to the Farsi.
    </p>
    <p class="task">salam, khoobi? see you at 5, merci</p>
    <div class="phone" role="group" aria-label="Simulated phone messaging screen">
      <div class="phone-screen" aria-live="polite">
        <span class="bubble" id="t3-bubble"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="predict" id="t3-pred" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="lang">EN</span><span>the</span><span>I</span><span>see</span>
      </div>
      <div class="phone-input">
        <input
          type="text"
          id="t3-in"
          autocomplete="off"
          autocapitalize="off"
          spellcheck="false"
          placeholder="type a word, then space"
          aria-label="Message"
        />
        <button type="button" class="piece-btn piece-btn-quiet" id="t3-send">Send</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="counter">
      <div><b id="c-words">0</b>words typed</div>
      <div><b id="c-fix">0</b>changed by autocorrect</div>
      <div><b id="c-sw">0</b>times the prediction language flipped</div>
    </div>
    <div class="reveal" id="t3-rev" hidden>
      <p>
        Every Farsi word became a different English word. A dyslexic writer often cannot tell which
        word autocorrect replaced, because the replacement looks about as plausible as what they typed.
      </p>
      <div class="finding">
        <b>What research says</b>
        Adults with dyslexia make more spelling errors in a second language and type more slowly, less
        accurately, with more visual checking (<em>Annals of Dyslexia</em>, 2025; <em>Reading and
        Writing</em>, 2025). Dyslexic error patterns differ by language (<em>Language Resources and
        Evaluation</em>, 2022). Keyboard, autocorrect and predictions each guess the language
        separately. Nobody owns the switch.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="adds-up">
    <h2 id="adds-up" class="hl">What this adds up to</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Dyslexia does not transfer between scripts. Accessibility settings should be per language, not per account.</li>
      <li>The switch moment is unowned. Keyboard, autocorrect and predictions need one shared active-language signal.</li>
      <li>Each script breaks at a different step: decoding, spelling, picking. One dyslexia mode cannot cover all three.</li>
      <li>No peer-reviewed study has asked bilingual adults with dyslexia about their phones. That is the study to run next.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</Piece>

<script>
  const $ = <T extends HTMLElement = HTMLElement>(id: string) => document.getElementById(id) as T;

  // Trial 1: time how long one vowel-less sentence took.
  const started = Date.now();
  const t1 = $<HTMLButtonElement>('t1-btn');
  t1.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const secs = Math.max(1, Math.round((Date.now() - started) / 1000));
    $('t1-time').textContent = `You spent about ${secs} second${secs === 1 ? '' : 's'} on one sentence.`;
    $('t1-rev').hidden = false;
    t1.disabled = true;
  });

  // Trial 2: pick the character; a right answer opens the next step.
  function wire(groupId: string, msgId: string, next: string, rightText: string) {
    let done = false;
    $(groupId).addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      const btn = (event.target as HTMLElement).closest<HTMLButtonElement>('.choice');
      if (!btn || done) return;
      const msg = $(msgId);
      if (btn.dataset.v === '1') {
        done = true;
        btn.classList.add('is-right');
        btn.setAttribute('aria-label', `${btn.textContent}, correct`);
        msg.textContent = rightText;
        $(next).hidden = false;
      } else {
        btn.classList.add('is-wrong');
        btn.setAttribute('aria-label', `${btn.textContent}, wrong`);
        msg.textContent = 'That one means something else. Try again.';
      }
    });
  }
  wire('t2-a', 't2-m1', 't2-rev', 'Right. Now try the next one.');
  wire('t2-b', 't2-m2', 't2-rev2', 'Right.');

  // Trial 3: English autocorrect meets Farsi written in Latin letters.
  const fixes: Record<string, string> = {
    salam: 'salad',
    khoobi: 'hobby',
    merci: 'mercy',
    chetori: 'cheerio',
    mamnoon: 'monsoon',
    khodafez: 'khakis',
  };
  const preds = { en: ['the', 'I', 'see'], fa: ['salam', 'khoobi', 'merci'] };
  let words = 0;
  let fixed = 0;
  let flips = 0;
  let lastLang: 'en' | 'fa' = 'en';
  let sent = false;

  const input = $<HTMLInputElement>('t3-in');
  const bubble = $('t3-bubble');
  const pred = $('t3-pred');

  function setPred(lang: 'en' | 'fa') {
    if (lang !== lastLang) {
      flips++;
      lastLang = lang;
      $('c-sw').textContent = String(flips);
    }
    pred.replaceChildren(
      Object.assign(document.createElement('span'), { className: 'lang', textContent: lang.toUpperCase() }),
      ...preds[lang].map((w) => Object.assign(document.createElement('span'), { textContent: w })),
    );
  }

  function commit(word: string) {
    if (!word) return;
    words++;
    $('c-words').textContent = String(words);
    const bare = word.toLowerCase().replace(/[,.?!]/g, '');
    const punct = word.slice(bare.length);
    if (bubble.childNodes.length) bubble.append(' ');
    if (fixes[bare]) {
      fixed++;
      $('c-fix').textContent = String(fixed);
      bubble.append(Object.assign(document.createElement('span'), { className: 'fix', textContent: fixes[bare] }), punct);
    } else {
      bubble.append(word);
    }
    setPred(bare in fixes ? 'fa' : 'en');
  }

  function send() {
    if (sent) return;
    commit(input.value.trim());
    input.value = '';
    sent = true;
    input.disabled = true;
    $<HTMLButtonElement>('t3-send').disabled = true;
    if (!bubble.childNodes.length) bubble.textContent = '(empty message)';
    $('t3-rev').hidden = false;
  }

  input.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
    if (sent) return;
    if (event.key === ' ') {
      event.preventDefault();
      commit(input.value.trim());
      input.value = '';
    }
    if (event.key === 'Enter') {
      event.preventDefault();
      send();
    }
  });

  // Phones often send no space keydown, so a typed space in the value counts too.
  input.addEventListener('input', () => {
    if (sent) return;
    if (input.value.endsWith(' ')) {
      commit(input.value.trim());
      input.value = '';
      return;
    }
    const v = input.value.trim().toLowerCase();
    if (v.length > 1) {
      const lang = pred.querySelector('.lang');
      if (lang) lang.textContent = Object.keys(fixes).some((w) => w.startsWith(v)) ? 'FA?' : 'EN';
    }
  });

  $('t3-send').addEventListener('click', send);
</script>
